  "prompt": "You are an associate analyst at Marshfield Croft Partners covering the healthcare sector. Johnson & Johnson filed its Q1 2026 10-Q on May 4, and Managing Director Lin Wei-chen has asked you to build a budget-vs-actual variance analysis ahead of the internal portfolio review meeting.\n\nPull all actual Q1 2026 figures directly from the EDGAR filing — specifically RevenueFromContractWithCustomerExcludingAssessedTax ($24,062M), CostOfGoodsAndServicesSold ($8,106M), GrossProfit ($15,956M), NetIncomeLoss ($5,235M), and SellingGeneralAndAdministrativeExpense ($6,034M). For the budget baseline, construct a consensus proxy from sell-side estimates available as of the filing date and document your sourcing methodology. If exact consensus figures aren't available, note the approximation approach in a sourcing notes section.\n\nThe deliverable is an Excel workbook with two tabs. The first tab (\"Actual vs Budget\") should follow standard sell-side template structure: line items in rows, with columns for actual, budget, absolute variance ($), percentage variance (%), favorable/unfavorable classification from an earnings perspective, and commentary. All monetary figures in millions USD, one decimal place. Flag any variance exceeding ±5% as material and provide a narrative explanation for each. Include a column tying every actual figure back to its EDGAR source (filing date, XBRL tag). Use our standard color scheme — blue font for inputs, black for formulas, red for variances. The second tab (\"Summary\") should serve as a dashboard with the three most significant variances highlighted and brief driver commentary.\n\nBeyond the line-item analysis, Lin wants you to address operating leverage by walking through how the revenue variance flows down through COGS and SG&A to the operating income line. Note that the pinned OperatingIncomeLoss figure ($5,827M) is from the 2015-03-29 filing period, not Q1 2026 — flag this period mismatch explicitly rather than blending it in. Similarly, the R&D expense fact on file ($109M, XBRL tag from 2025-12-28) is a fiscal year-end figure, not a quarterly amount for Q1 2026; call out that discrepancy clearly. For directional trend context, note that total Revenues were $56,077M as reported under the historical XBRL tag in 2014, but this is a different revenue concept (Revenues vs. RevenueFromContractWithCustomerExcludingAssessedTax) — reconcile and flag this so downstream readers aren't misled. The attached JNJ_historical_financials.xlsx provides five-year historical financials you can reference for trend commentary.\n\nSet print areas, include headers/footers with our confidentiality notice, and protect the workbook except for designated input cells.",
